  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 109 AM CDT Sat Jul 25 2026\n\nA strong upper level ridge will persist across the high plains\nthrough Tuesday.The various ensemble means continue to indicate\n700 and 850mb temperatures in the extreme upper end of the\nclimatological spectrum, similar to those of 2011 and 2012.\nThe only difference is that soils were drier in those years and\nthere was much less greeunp due to drought. This may keep \ntemperatures from reaching to levels of those years. However, \nsome locations could reach 107-109 Monday. Expect very high heat\nindices today due to the combination of heat and high moisture \ncontent. By Sunday and Monday as we dry out due to deep mixing,\nit will be hotter but drier so that heat indices remain about \nthe same. An extreme heat warning is in effect for Barber County\nfor today with a heat advisory for the remainder of western \nKansas. A heat advisory is in effect for Sunday and Monday, and\nthis may need an upgrade to an extreme heat warning across \ncentral Kansas depending on exactly how hot it gets and how much\nthe air dries out.\n\nA strong shortwave trough in northwest flow across the Great\nLakes will push a cold front into the central plains by Monday \nevening despite the very high 500mb heights. This will result \nin lower temperatures but much higher humidity, with dewpoints \nnear or above 70 degrees. Thunderstorms are possible as early as\nMonday evening along the front across west central Kansas and \nthe I-70 corridor. Given the non-existent mid to upper level \nwinds, these will be mainly sub-severe. But when it's 105-108 \ndegrees on Monday, it won't take a very robust t-storm to get \nmarginally severe winds up to 60 mph. The front will remain \nstalled out across southwest Kansas through next week, with \nhotter conditions along the KS/OK line and cooler but humid \nconditions along I-70. The exact location of the front will \ndetermine heat and humidity levels as well as t-storm chances.",
